"""The sectors the Bay city is built in, one small piece at a time. Not run on its own.

A sector is built, looked at from opposite angles, fixed, and only then is the next one started. Its area is
either a rectangle, "box": (west, east, south, north) in metres, or an "outline": corner points, counter-
clockwise, for a sector whose sides follow streets that do not run north-south or east-west (its box is then
worked out from the outline). Sector meshes meet exactly along shared sides, so neighbours must share their
boundary points. Put a sector's sides where they cut no block in two: inside a carriageway, or half a metre
behind the back of a sidewalk (the property line).

Optional keys
  water   polygons that are open water whatever the map says (straightens a quay)
  fill    polygons that are dry ground whatever the map says (a quay apron, a pier); applied after "water"
  open    polygons where no building may stand (promenades, squares, parks)
  plazas  polygons furnished as a square: trees in a grid, benches, lamps (plan_props.py); also kept open
  parks   polygons of lawn (material slot Grass); streets and sidewalks cut them; trees stand straight in the grass
  aprons  polygons of asphalt that is not a street: block ground in the material slot Road, level with the
          carriageway, so a drive without kerbs (class "alley") runs straight on to it (a training ground, a car
          park). Streets and sidewalks cut them; no building stands on one
  paint   lines painted on the aprons: {"colour": "white" or "yellow", "line": [points], "width": metres
          (default 0.12), "dash": (on, off) metres}; paint only shows on an apron or a carriageway
  fences  panels of a fence kit end to end along a line (plan_props.py): {"line": [points], "meshes": [panel
          models, picked at random], "panel_m": a panel's length (default 4.03), "post": model of the post that
          closes a run, "gaps": [[(x, y), (x, y)], ...] stretches of the line left open (a gate)}
  pieces  single things with a place of their own: (model, (x, y), Unreal yaw in degrees, scale)
  furniture  "lamps": the sector's streets get their lamps and nothing else: no parking meters, hydrants, bins,
          mailboxes, news racks or street trees (a road out of town)
  park_tree_m2  square metres of lawn per tree (default in plan_props.py); larger for a wide open lawn
  park_bench_m  metres of lawn edge per bench (default 25)
  benches a bench every so often along a line, all looking the same way: {"line": [points], "every": metres
          (default 9), "side": "left" or "right" of the line's direction} (a belvedere's rim, a promenade)
  wild    natural ground: True (the whole sector) or polygons. Block ground left as the country is, in a material
          painted like the landscape round the city (slot Wild) and planted like it (plan_country.py, not
          plan_props.py). Streets, sidewalks, lawns and gardens cut it; gardens only reach 11 m from a building.
          Where it reaches the sector's own side the landscape carries on from its edge (build_surface.py)
  no_auto polygons where only the sector's ranks and landmarks are built: no ordinary street fronts
  yards   False: no gardens. In a district of walk-up rows, what the buildings leave over inside the blocks
          becomes lawn with trees (plan_yards.py); a sector that lays out its own lawns switches that off
  yard_tree_m2  square metres of garden per tree (default in plan_props.py)
  landmarks  buildings with a fixed place: (model, (x, y), Unreal yaw in degrees, scale); set before any other
  max_height_m  no other building of the sector taller than this (keeps a landmark the tallest around it)
  parapet True: a low wall runs along the top of every sea wall and retaining wall (a high terrace), not bollards
  walls   segments of the outline where the sector's ground ends above natural ground: a retaining wall there
  barriers  walls standing on the ground: {"line": [points], "height": m, "width": m}; leave a gap for a gate
            (wall() below makes one with a coping and piers). With "lift": m the piece starts that far above
            the ground (a coping, a lintel); with "level": True it is one height from end to end
  levels  ground held at one height along given lines, as if a level street ran there (a walled yard, a
          terrace): {"z": street-surface height, "lines": [[points], ...]}. A block's ground is carried by the
          streets round it; these lines carry it too. Keep them 10 m away from a street that is not at that height
  relief  areas whose ground follows the natural relief (terrain_base) instead of being carried flat by the
          streets far away: a hillside park, a valley floor with a knoll: [{"area": [points], "step": metres
          between rows of samples (default 16), "clear": metres kept free beside each kerb (default 12), "blend":
          metres over which the streets' height eases into the natural one (default 20), "lines": [[points]]
          sampled every 6 m (an open side; a seam, given alike in both sectors), "skip": [[points]] where the
          relief read from the drawing is not ground (a symbol's shading): no samples there}]
  over_tunnels  names of tunnels (structures.TUNNELS) under the sector: along both sides of each the ground is
          held above the roof slab where the tunnel is covered, and no lower than the road beside its open ramps
  Scripts that need the sector's ground heights take "levels" through levels(name), which adds what "relief"
  and "over_tunnels" give and the seams along the sector's sides; its outline they take through outline(name),
  which adds the seams' points.
Seams (the list SEAMS, below the sectors): a side shared by two sectors on open ground, or open to the country,
gets the same points and the same heights in every sector it runs along. Without one, a side with only its two
ends is a straight rail between their heights whatever the ground does. Tools/bay/audit_seams.py measures every
side of every built sector; seamed() names the sectors to make again after a seam is added or changed.
  ranks   buildings of one kind in a line: {"line": [a, b], "units": [models], "scale": 1.0}; fronts on the
          line, bodies on its left; a block that holds a rank takes nothing else (plan_lots.py)
  views   extra review cameras, (label, (x, y) to stand on, (x, y) to look at), for plan_views.py
status: "planned", "roads" (street surface built and reviewed), "built" (buildings, furniture and trees in
and reviewed from opposite angles; open items are listed in the vault), "done" (nothing left open).
"""
import math


def oval(centre, a, b, first_deg, last_deg, steps=24):
    """Points along an ellipse (half-axes a east-west, b north-south), angle from east, counter-clockwise.
    With first and last 360 degrees apart the result is a closed ring; otherwise an arc, closed by its chord."""
    span = last_deg - first_deg
    count = steps if abs(span) >= 360.0 else steps + 1
    return [(round(centre[0] + a * math.cos(math.radians(first_deg + span * k / steps)), 2),
             round(centre[1] + b * math.sin(math.radians(first_deg + span * k / steps)), 2)) for k in range(count)]


def wall(line, height, width, pier_every=0.0, pier=1.4, pier_rise=0.5, cap=0.15):
    """A wall for a sector's "barriers": the wall along `line`, a coping on top that overhangs it by `cap` on
    each side (0: none), a square pier on each of its ends and corners, and more piers along it about every
    `pier_every` metres (0: none). A pier stands `pier_rise` above the wall."""
    out = [{"line": [tuple(p) for p in line], "height": height, "width": width}]
    if cap > 0.0:
        out.append({"line": [tuple(p) for p in line], "height": 0.25, "width": width + 2.0 * cap, "lift": height - 0.05})
    spots = [tuple(p) for p in line]
    if pier_every > 0.0:
        for (ax, ay), (bx, by) in zip(line[:-1], line[1:]):
            run = math.hypot(bx - ax, by - ay)
            count = int(round(run / pier_every))
            spots += [(ax + (bx - ax) * k / count, ay + (by - ay) * k / count) for k in range(1, count)]
    for x, y in spots:
        out.append({"line": [(round(x - pier / 2.0, 3), round(y, 3)), (round(x + pier / 2.0, 3), round(y, 3))],
                    "height": height + pier_rise, "width": pier})
    return out


def run(a, b, step=30.0):
    """Points from a towards b, about `step` metres apart, b left out: a long side of an outline that follows no
    street gets vertices along it, so the ground mesh follows the ground there and a wall on it comes in panels."""
    count = max(1, int(round(math.hypot(b[0] - a[0], b[1] - a[1]) / step)))
    return [(round(a[0] + (b[0] - a[0]) * k / count, 2), round(a[1] + (b[1] - a[1]) * k / count, 2)) for k in range(count)]


def square_lawns(corners, path_m=6.0, place_r=15.0, steps=10):
    """A public square laid out on a whole block: two walks from corner to corner and a round paved place where
    they cross. Returns (the four lawns between them, the ring of the round place) for a sector's "parks" and
    "plazas". corners: the block's four corners in order (street junctions will do: streets and sidewalks cut
    the lawns back to the property line). The walks are what is left between the lawns: plain paving."""
    (ax, ay), (bx, by), (cx, cy), (dx, dy) = corners
    den = (cx - ax) * (dy - by) - (cy - ay) * (dx - bx)
    k = ((bx - ax) * (dy - by) - (by - ay) * (dx - bx)) / den
    ox, oy = ax + k * (cx - ax), ay + k * (cy - ay)              # where the two diagonals cross
    h = path_m / 2.0

    def unit(vx, vy):
        size = math.hypot(vx, vy)
        return vx / size, vy / size

    def lawn(p, q):
        dp, dq = unit(p[0] - ox, p[1] - oy), unit(q[0] - ox, q[1] - oy)
        # each walk's edge on this lawn's side: the diagonal moved h towards the other corner
        np_ = (-dp[1], dp[0]) if -dp[1] * dq[0] + dp[0] * dq[1] > 0 else (dp[1], -dp[0])
        nq = (-dq[1], dq[0]) if -dq[1] * dp[0] + dq[0] * dp[1] > 0 else (dq[1], -dq[0])
        sx, sy = q[0] - p[0], q[1] - p[1]

        def on_side(d, n):
            x0, y0 = ox + n[0] * h, oy + n[1] * h
            run = ((p[0] - x0) * sy - (p[1] - y0) * sx) / (d[0] * sy - d[1] * sx)
            return x0 + run * d[0], y0 + run * d[1]

        def on_place(d, n):
            run = math.sqrt(place_r ** 2 - h ** 2)
            return ox + n[0] * h + run * d[0], oy + n[1] * h + run * d[1]

        first, last = on_place(dq, nq), on_place(dp, np_)
        a0 = math.atan2(first[1] - oy, first[0] - ox)
        turn = (math.atan2(last[1] - oy, last[0] - ox) - a0 + math.pi) % (2.0 * math.pi) - math.pi
        arc = [(ox + place_r * math.cos(a0 + turn * i / steps), oy + place_r * math.sin(a0 + turn * i / steps)) for i in range(steps + 1)]
        return [(round(x, 2), round(y, 2)) for x, y in [on_side(dp, np_), on_side(dq, nq)] + arc]

    ring = [corners[i] for i in range(4)]
    lawns = [lawn(ring[i], ring[(i + 1) % 4]) for i in range(4)]
    return lawns, oval((round(ox, 2), round(oy, 2)), place_r, place_r, 0.0, 360.0)


